WEBVTT

00:01:10.550 --> 00:01:14.570
<b>1965
Summer...</b>

00:01:38.210 --> 00:01:39.880
They're dumping here again.

00:01:48.170 --> 00:01:50.120
Damn it.

00:01:53.110 --> 00:01:54.360
Those pests.

00:01:56.090 --> 00:01:57.180
Hey!

00:01:57.180 --> 00:01:58.090
What the hell are you doing?!

00:02:00.830 --> 00:02:02.920
This is city property!

00:02:02.920 --> 00:02:04.330
Dumping trash here is—

00:02:37.330 --> 00:02:39.050
This was how it began.

00:02:42.980 --> 00:02:46.650
<b>Episode 14</b>

00:02:42.980 --> 00:02:46.650
<b>A New Chapter of the Rakshasa</b>

00:02:47.820 --> 00:02:50.010
The buried corpse was that of a young man.

00:02:50.550 --> 00:02:52.440
With no significant marks on the body,

00:02:52.440 --> 00:02:56.120
the cause of death was only 
determined later through autopsy.

00:03:13.450 --> 00:03:14.900
The autopsy revealed 
that the cause of death

00:03:14.900 --> 00:03:17.710
had been shock induced 
by loss of blood.

00:03:17.710 --> 00:03:21.040
However, there was no 
visible wound that could

00:03:21.040 --> 00:03:23.210
have explained the fatal loss of blood.

00:03:23.210 --> 00:03:25.700
This was the only thing found.

00:03:25.700 --> 00:03:28.700
A small wound on the left forearm,
resembling one made by a syringe.

00:03:28.700 --> 00:03:31.680
The coroner determined this was the 
opening from which the blood was drawn.

00:03:31.680 --> 00:03:35.080
The investigatory headquarters 
could not say if this had been homicide

00:03:35.080 --> 00:03:37.120
or some kind of medical 
procedure gone wrong.

00:03:37.120 --> 00:03:40.820
The official announcement was 
that the young man had died

00:03:40.820 --> 00:03:44.800
due to a massive amount of blood 
loss, drawn from his left forearm.

00:03:44.800 --> 00:03:46.160
A very peculiar death.

00:03:47.780 --> 00:03:49.170
<b>Missing Persons Incidents Continue</b>

00:03:48.600 --> 00:03:51.630
The media jumped on the 
bandwagon, and for months,

00:03:50.750 --> 00:03:52.170
<b>A Vampire Appears!</b>

00:03:51.630 --> 00:03:55.670
these young missing persons were 
a great piece of sensationalism.

00:03:57.170 --> 00:03:59.310
Excuse me, Yasuoka-san.

00:04:00.880 --> 00:04:02.310
It's about that incident the other day...

00:04:03.810 --> 00:04:05.050
Can you just come with me?

00:04:05.050 --> 00:04:07.180
Y-Yes.

00:04:19.230 --> 00:04:20.190
What do you think?

00:04:23.510 --> 00:04:25.070
That's him, no doubt.

00:04:26.090 --> 00:04:27.850
It's Hirayama Yukio.

00:04:31.750 --> 00:04:33.670
Senpai, do you...

00:04:33.670 --> 00:04:34.580
Huh?

00:04:34.970 --> 00:04:37.350
Do you have any idea who did it?

00:04:37.730 --> 00:04:39.140
Hey, hey!

00:04:39.140 --> 00:04:40.810
How the hell would I?

00:04:40.810 --> 00:04:42.220
Why me?

00:04:44.100 --> 00:04:48.210
Hirayama Yukio had been a rep player 
for one of the yakuza groups,

00:04:48.210 --> 00:04:51.130
and Yasuoka had once been his manager.

00:04:51.930 --> 00:04:55.600
If only for a short time, 
they had fought side by side.

00:04:56.240 --> 00:04:57.320
That's right.

00:04:57.320 --> 00:04:59.840
Hirayama was the fake Akagi.

00:05:03.290 --> 00:05:07.210
And right then, 
Yasuoka had an epiphany.

00:05:07.980 --> 00:05:11.430
A thought that had gradually 
come to coalesce in his mind.

00:05:11.430 --> 00:05:12.270
That's right.

00:05:12.270 --> 00:05:15.120
He had a clue about 
these mysterious deaths.

00:05:15.700 --> 00:05:17.750
<b>Inada Group Second in Command
Ougi</b>

00:05:18.100 --> 00:05:20.460
I'm sorry I made you come all 
the way out here, Ougi-san.

00:05:22.260 --> 00:05:26.260
It's not every day I get a 
call from a police officer.

00:05:26.950 --> 00:05:29.190
So why did you call?

00:05:29.190 --> 00:05:32.120
Why don't we have a drink first?

00:05:38.050 --> 00:05:41.040
I know it's sudden, 
but it's about that.

00:05:43.160 --> 00:05:44.160
I won't deny it.

00:05:44.160 --> 00:05:49.600
Our group was involved and introduced 
a few young guys to the old man.

00:05:49.600 --> 00:05:54.590
Outcasts and youngsters that 
nobody would bother looking for.

00:05:54.590 --> 00:05:58.160
But the stakes for these games 
were always outrageously high.

00:05:58.160 --> 00:06:00.310
And then, there were special rules.

00:06:00.310 --> 00:06:02.600
Even a skilled person 
would have trouble

00:06:02.600 --> 00:06:05.340
keeping a clear head under 
those circumstances.

00:06:05.770 --> 00:06:08.810
It's a game of Mahjong someone 
poor could never win.

00:06:08.810 --> 00:06:12.680
But lately, there haven't 
been any requests.

00:06:16.110 --> 00:06:18.910
Maybe the old man got 
sick of this game.

00:06:18.910 --> 00:06:20.350
I'm not so sure about that.

00:06:21.200 --> 00:06:24.740
Around when did he stop calling you?

00:06:24.740 --> 00:06:27.270
Four, five months ago?

00:06:27.270 --> 00:06:28.590
I knew it.

00:06:28.590 --> 00:06:29.380
Huh?

00:06:29.380 --> 00:06:36.190
It fits completely with the string 
of young people disappearing lately.

00:06:36.820 --> 00:06:39.550
What this means is that 
the old man's little hobby

00:06:39.550 --> 00:06:42.200
has intensified and he's a monster now.

00:06:42.960 --> 00:06:46.270
That corpse they found on Mount Tama 
was just one of his victims.

00:06:47.890 --> 00:06:48.870
Ougi-san.

00:06:49.210 --> 00:06:54.120
I'm thinking of hunting down this 
old man... I mean, this monster.

00:06:54.120 --> 00:06:56.880
With our own monster.

00:06:56.880 --> 00:06:58.150
Monster?

00:06:58.540 --> 00:06:59.580
Yes.

00:06:59.580 --> 00:07:02.150
We'll pit him against the 
monster Akagi Shigeru.

00:07:03.840 --> 00:07:06.910
They say the old man is 
worth about one billion.

00:07:06.910 --> 00:07:10.660
If Akagi wins, we'll split 
it between us three ways.

00:07:12.780 --> 00:07:17.610
One billion yen in 1965 would 
be roughly ten billion yen today.

00:07:18.180 --> 00:07:20.170
Akagi Shigeru?

00:07:20.170 --> 00:07:21.910
Couldn't find him.

00:07:23.380 --> 00:07:25.370
I tried asking around

00:07:25.370 --> 00:07:29.910
about a rep player fitting that 
description, but there was none.

00:07:29.910 --> 00:07:33.210
There is one rumor that's 
caught my attention, though.

00:07:33.210 --> 00:07:37.560
Supposedly there's this young guy 
who keeps winning every single game

00:07:37.560 --> 00:07:40.180
at one of our affiliate 
group's chou-han operations.

00:07:37.560 --> 00:07:40.180
Chou-han - a game where players bet 
on the even/odd outcome of two dice.

00:07:40.870 --> 00:07:44.220
Some twenty or so year old punk.

00:07:44.220 --> 00:07:46.310
He takes on a lot of crazy bets,

00:07:46.310 --> 00:07:48.200
but he's just some wash-out with no backup.

00:07:48.910 --> 00:07:51.940
Based on your description, 
that just might be him.

00:07:55.510 --> 00:07:56.540
A few days later,

00:07:56.950 --> 00:08:00.540
Yasuoka and Ougi were on their way 
to meet the Kurata Group in Chiba

00:08:01.130 --> 00:08:05.780
to confirm if the young man on the 
winning streak was, indeed, Akagi.

00:08:05.780 --> 00:08:12.000
If it is not him, Yasuoka's plans 
will all go down the drain.

00:08:15.840 --> 00:08:18.830
<b>Inside the Kurata Group's Gambling House</b>

00:08:17.530 --> 00:08:20.080
Just then, the Kurata Group, 
who had lost about 20 million yen

00:08:20.080 --> 00:08:26.280
in the three months to this young man, 
were about to take some drastic measures.

00:08:27.390 --> 00:08:33.080
20 million yen would be worth 
close to 200 million yen today.

00:08:33.080 --> 00:08:34.360
All right.

00:08:34.360 --> 00:08:36.240
Listen up.

00:08:36.240 --> 00:08:43.120
Everybody in this room, myself included, 
agrees that this roll was odd with 4 and 5.

00:08:43.120 --> 00:08:45.850
You're the only one left.

00:08:45.850 --> 00:08:50.860
As long as you don't babble 
nonsense, it'll all go down well.

00:08:50.860 --> 00:08:54.000
The young man was Akagi Shigeru.

00:08:57.010 --> 00:09:02.160
Saying the wrong thing will 
only insult these people here.

00:09:02.160 --> 00:09:06.040
So think about that before you answer.

00:09:06.040 --> 00:09:10.040
Is the roll even or odd?

00:09:15.980 --> 00:09:18.000
<i>I know you know what I'm talking about.</i>

00:09:19.050 --> 00:09:23.870
<i>I'm not asking you whether 
this roll is even or odd.</i>

00:09:24.560 --> 00:09:28.060
<i>What I'm asking about is your life.</i>

00:09:28.060 --> 00:09:32.600
<i>I'm asking if you want to live, 
or if you want to die.</i>

00:09:41.080 --> 00:09:42.710
Even if there was a monster

00:09:44.190 --> 00:09:51.030
that controlled this country... 
no, the entire world,

00:09:51.540 --> 00:09:55.250
even this person could not twist the truth.

00:09:57.270 --> 00:10:01.550
The truth that everybody has to die, 
and the outcome of a gamble!

00:10:03.320 --> 00:10:04.060
It's even.

00:10:06.760 --> 00:10:07.930
You little bastard!

00:10:07.930 --> 00:10:09.830
How long are you going to screw around?!

00:10:20.650 --> 00:10:22.930
I'm only going to ask you one more time.

00:10:22.930 --> 00:10:24.670
This is your last chance.

00:10:24.670 --> 00:10:30.250
Pick the wrong words 
and you'll lose your head.

00:10:30.600 --> 00:10:35.260
Is this even or odd?

00:11:08.960 --> 00:11:09.690
Even.

00:11:13.730 --> 00:11:15.070
I see.

00:11:15.700 --> 00:11:18.470
Then die!

00:11:19.230 --> 00:11:20.470
Hold it!

00:11:25.110 --> 00:11:26.320
Can't you hear that?

00:11:26.320 --> 00:11:27.620
We have a guest.

00:11:29.030 --> 00:11:30.790
Is anyone there?

00:11:35.690 --> 00:11:36.750
Go.

00:11:38.410 --> 00:11:40.160
Yes, sir.

00:11:59.500 --> 00:12:01.150
We want to play.

00:12:01.150 --> 00:12:03.130
The gambling house is open, right?

00:12:04.090 --> 00:12:07.250
I'm sorry, but we closed early today.

00:12:08.060 --> 00:12:08.750
You're closed?

00:12:09.340 --> 00:12:11.750
I am terribly sorry, since you've 
come all this way, but...

00:12:12.610 --> 00:12:15.300
If you could please leave now.

00:12:18.540 --> 00:12:19.550
Hey!

00:12:20.570 --> 00:12:21.760
What the hell is up with him?

00:12:22.110 --> 00:12:24.240
Talk about abrupt.

00:12:24.760 --> 00:12:25.670
What do you want to do?

00:12:25.670 --> 00:12:26.860
Shall we just go?

00:12:28.020 --> 00:12:28.670
<i>Blood.</i>

00:12:29.900 --> 00:12:30.670
What's wrong?

00:12:31.140 --> 00:12:32.270
Yasuoka!

00:12:35.680 --> 00:12:36.650
Hey!

00:12:36.650 --> 00:12:37.780
Where do you think you're going?

00:12:39.310 --> 00:12:40.180
<i>Akagi!</i>

00:12:40.880 --> 00:12:42.680
Akagi!

00:12:53.330 --> 00:12:54.160
Akagi...

00:12:54.160 --> 00:12:55.780
Who the hell are you?

00:12:55.780 --> 00:12:56.650
You bastard...

00:12:56.650 --> 00:12:57.650
Wait.

00:12:58.970 --> 00:13:00.740
You're Ougi Takeshi-san, am I right?

00:13:01.200 --> 00:13:02.400
You in the back.

00:13:02.980 --> 00:13:05.620
Both our groups are subgroups 
of the Santou Group.

00:13:05.620 --> 00:13:07.890
You're the second in command 
for the Inada Group.

00:13:07.890 --> 00:13:09.430
That is you, right?

00:13:10.410 --> 00:13:12.330
It's been too long.

00:13:12.330 --> 00:13:15.410
I apologize for barging in like this.

00:13:15.410 --> 00:13:17.770
I really do.

00:13:17.770 --> 00:13:18.920
But what is this?

00:13:20.510 --> 00:13:23.420
It seems you witnessed 
something unsightly.

00:13:23.970 --> 00:13:29.430
We have to use force so rarely, 
but this guy just doesn't listen.

00:13:29.920 --> 00:13:31.540
What a mess.

00:13:31.540 --> 00:13:34.430
You can see that the dice roll is even.

00:13:34.820 --> 00:13:38.170
But he keeps saying that it's odd.

00:13:38.780 --> 00:13:41.140
Of course, now that you're here,

00:13:41.140 --> 00:13:46.180
he'll probably say that he said it 
was even from the very start.

00:13:46.640 --> 00:13:48.180
<i>That's a bald-faced lie.</i>

00:13:48.770 --> 00:13:51.100
<i>Akagi is a true pariah.</i>

00:13:51.100 --> 00:13:52.440
<i>An outcast.</i>

00:13:52.440 --> 00:13:55.470
<i>He's a heretic who spits on society's rules.</i>

00:13:56.190 --> 00:13:59.510
<i>But he wouldn't change his 
mind in such a manner.</i>

00:13:59.510 --> 00:14:01.640
<i>Even if his life depended on it.</i>

00:14:02.270 --> 00:14:05.700
<i>He didn't bet odd.</i>

00:14:06.330 --> 00:14:07.700
<i>He bet even.</i>

00:14:08.390 --> 00:14:13.060
<i>And now, the Kurata Group is trying 
to change this fact using force.</i>

00:14:13.700 --> 00:14:15.900
<i>The Kurata Group won't 
back down from this.</i>

00:14:15.900 --> 00:14:17.490
<i>They simply can't.</i>

00:14:17.960 --> 00:14:19.410
<i>But...</i>

00:14:19.410 --> 00:14:21.260
<i>Akagi won't withdraw, either.</i>

00:14:21.260 --> 00:14:24.190
<i>He's the type who sees it 
through to the bitter end.</i>

00:14:26.380 --> 00:14:27.580
<i>What should I do?</i>

00:14:27.580 --> 00:14:29.070
<i>What can I do?</i>

00:14:29.960 --> 00:14:32.730
<i>What we're facing is the 
very essence of gambling.</i>

00:14:32.730 --> 00:14:34.210
<i>A meaningless death.</i>

00:14:34.810 --> 00:14:36.020
<i>Ridiculous!</i>

00:14:36.020 --> 00:14:38.690
<i>There's no way I can let 
an unparalleled talent</i>

00:14:38.690 --> 00:14:41.460
<i>like Akagi get snuffed out in a 
pathetic gambling hole like this.</i>

00:14:41.890 --> 00:14:46.270
<i>That's so stupid that 
it simply can't happen!</i>

00:14:49.390 --> 00:14:52.640
So, if you would be so kind as 
to take your leave for today.

00:15:03.540 --> 00:15:04.890
The roll is gone!

00:15:04.890 --> 00:15:06.550
I just got rid of it!

00:15:07.500 --> 00:15:09.060
There's no odd or even.

00:15:09.490 --> 00:15:13.060
This thing you all put your pride 
on the line for is gone now!

00:15:13.060 --> 00:15:15.040
You bastard, what do you 
think you're doing?!

00:15:15.040 --> 00:15:17.130
Manager, please stop this.

00:15:17.130 --> 00:15:19.170
This isn't any of your business!

00:15:19.170 --> 00:15:21.070
That's not true.

00:15:21.570 --> 00:15:24.410
Yasuoka and I are like brothers.

00:15:24.410 --> 00:15:26.030
I can't just look the other way.

00:15:26.030 --> 00:15:29.970
When you lift a blade 
against Yasuoka or Akagi,

00:15:29.970 --> 00:15:34.060
you lift that blade against me, 
and therefore the Inada Group.

00:15:34.060 --> 00:15:36.080
That's how I'm forced to interpret this.

00:15:36.080 --> 00:15:37.320
Manager!

00:15:37.320 --> 00:15:41.820
Is there any way we can pretend 
that this bet never even happened?

00:15:42.040 --> 00:15:43.690
Is this a joke?

00:15:43.690 --> 00:15:48.320
If you insist, then I will have no 
choice but to report this up the chain.

00:15:48.320 --> 00:15:50.060
Is that your wish?

00:15:53.430 --> 00:15:54.460
Akagi!

00:15:54.460 --> 00:15:55.720
Akagi!

00:15:55.720 --> 00:15:57.070
Hang on!

00:15:57.070 --> 00:15:58.510
Akagi!

00:15:58.510 --> 00:16:00.140
Akagi!

00:16:11.470 --> 00:16:12.680
How did it go?

00:16:15.070 --> 00:16:16.020
Nothing to worry about.

00:16:16.340 --> 00:16:20.070
A wound like that isn't fatal.

00:16:20.070 --> 00:16:21.730
So long as you stop the bleeding, that is.

00:16:28.170 --> 00:16:29.860
That was close, though.

00:16:29.860 --> 00:16:34.770
If we hadn't arrived when we did, 
he would've lost his life there.

00:16:35.370 --> 00:16:36.670
It's just like you said.

00:16:36.670 --> 00:16:38.760
He has absolutely no fear of death.

00:16:38.760 --> 00:16:41.870
He's someone who can look 
death in the eye as it takes him,

00:16:41.870 --> 00:16:44.780
rather than being wracked by fear.

00:16:45.430 --> 00:16:47.480
That's a rare type of man right there.

00:16:47.480 --> 00:16:49.780
He really is an irreplaceable asset.

00:16:49.780 --> 00:16:52.790
I couldn't let him die there.

00:16:52.790 --> 00:16:54.290
Right, Yasuoka?

00:16:54.290 --> 00:16:57.160
An enormous amount of 
money is riding on this.

00:16:57.160 --> 00:16:59.850
If he's going to die, he'll have 
to do it at the table where

00:16:59.850 --> 00:17:01.100
that money's being wagered.

00:17:03.490 --> 00:17:05.850
I'm glad it wasn't anything serious.

00:17:05.850 --> 00:17:08.310
They said none of your 
nerves were severed, either.

00:17:08.860 --> 00:17:11.510
You better make this the last time you

00:17:11.510 --> 00:17:14.510
try to throw away your life like that.

00:17:15.360 --> 00:17:21.320
Is there any point in standing on 
principle against scum like them?

00:17:27.050 --> 00:17:29.630
Sometimes, you just want to run 
straight ahead, without reason.

00:17:30.470 --> 00:17:32.830
That was one of those times.

00:17:33.530 --> 00:17:37.510
My own blood didn't allow 
me to bend my will.

00:17:38.040 --> 00:17:40.230
I would've rather died than give in.

00:17:40.230 --> 00:17:41.170
That was my true desire.

00:17:42.750 --> 00:17:45.570
The question is, what do you do 
with the life saved in that way?

00:17:46.090 --> 00:17:48.010
Until you figure that out,

00:17:48.010 --> 00:17:50.490
your life was only prolonged 
for the sake of prolonging it.

00:17:51.620 --> 00:17:53.940
It's better to just die 
when your time comes.

00:17:54.490 --> 00:17:56.850
It makes no difference if the 
other person is insane or not.

00:17:57.270 --> 00:17:59.190
If your time has come,

00:18:00.310 --> 00:18:01.770
you should just die.

00:18:03.130 --> 00:18:04.630
Wrong!

00:18:07.870 --> 00:18:11.630
Prolong your life, and you will 
tread where nobody has tread before.

00:18:12.190 --> 00:18:15.000
If you keep thinking like that, 
all you'll ever be is an outcast.

00:18:15.000 --> 00:18:20.210
A life that's decided by "talent" 
alone is a third-rate way of life.

00:18:20.820 --> 00:18:22.680
Why don't you build on it?

00:18:22.680 --> 00:18:25.660
If you'd only build on 
that talent of yours,

00:18:25.660 --> 00:18:27.210
the whole world could 
be yours for the taking.

00:18:27.900 --> 00:18:29.700
You could control it all.

00:18:29.700 --> 00:18:30.910
All of it!

00:18:33.390 --> 00:18:34.860
I'm not interested.

00:18:36.670 --> 00:18:37.860
Don't say that.

00:18:38.200 --> 00:18:42.080
You've got a glow ordinary people 
will never be able to achieve.

00:18:42.080 --> 00:18:43.610
You've got a certain light that they

00:18:43.610 --> 00:18:46.370
will never have, no matter 
how much they wish for it.

00:18:47.010 --> 00:18:51.940
It's true that you've won until now 
using nothing but your talents.

00:18:51.940 --> 00:18:56.380
But it also means you've been 
blessed with easy opponents.

00:18:56.970 --> 00:18:58.310
In other words,

00:18:58.310 --> 00:19:01.870
you've only ever come up against 
third and second rate players.

00:19:01.870 --> 00:19:03.880
No wonder you've 
become bored with life.

00:19:04.970 --> 00:19:10.890
You couldn't stand this tepid 
water you've been standing in.

00:19:13.230 --> 00:19:17.400
It's not a cold death you're 
wishing for, deep down.

00:19:18.170 --> 00:19:21.030
What you want is a life so boiling hot

00:19:21.030 --> 00:19:23.890
that ordinary people couldn't 
even bear to touch it.

00:19:23.890 --> 00:19:29.350
A scorching, scalding life.

00:19:29.350 --> 00:19:33.910
You need an opponent worthy of you.

00:19:36.110 --> 00:19:39.420
I know a person, Akagi.

00:19:42.380 --> 00:19:44.170
I should've seen that coming.

00:19:45.770 --> 00:19:49.520
You want me to play for you 
in some high-stakes game.

00:19:49.940 --> 00:19:51.930
Me against this guy.

00:19:53.450 --> 00:19:56.400
This whole speech was 
nothing but a preamble.

00:20:00.470 --> 00:20:02.910
Yasuoka, if you would...

00:20:02.910 --> 00:20:03.950
Yes.

00:20:05.650 --> 00:20:08.670
All the papers are talking about 
these freaky serial murders.

00:20:08.670 --> 00:20:11.870
The man behind them 
will be your opponent.

00:20:12.450 --> 00:20:17.070
You knew the man whose corpse 
they found over in Tama.

00:20:17.070 --> 00:20:18.460
This was him.

00:20:20.960 --> 00:20:22.350
That's...

00:20:22.350 --> 00:20:23.460
Yes.

00:20:23.460 --> 00:20:26.790
The fake who once assumed your name.

00:20:26.790 --> 00:20:29.470
He was my ex-partner, 
Hirayama Yukio.

00:20:29.470 --> 00:20:32.970
<b>Vampire Appears</b>

00:20:30.350 --> 00:20:33.790
As you know, he wasn't someone 
who'd put his life on the line.

00:20:33.010 --> 00:20:35.970
<b>Mysterious Death</b>

00:20:33.790 --> 00:20:35.970
He was most likely forced into it.

00:20:36.480 --> 00:20:41.480
This monster forced him to bet his 
own life and then he killed him.

00:20:41.480 --> 00:20:43.390
Do you get it, Akagi?

00:20:43.390 --> 00:20:46.480
This time, it's not just about money.

00:20:46.480 --> 00:20:48.480
You're going in to kill 
a man-eating tiger.

00:20:49.340 --> 00:20:49.990
It's a hunt.

00:20:50.520 --> 00:20:52.070
Hunt him down, Akagi!

00:20:52.070 --> 00:20:55.490
You're the only one who can 
stop this monster's rampage!

00:20:59.520 --> 00:21:02.670
This is the prologue for a 
new legend of Akagi Shigeru,

00:21:02.670 --> 00:21:04.820
the genius who descended 
into the darkness.

00:21:05.310 --> 00:21:08.590
The bell that marks its beginning 
has just been struck...

00:22:16.270 --> 00:22:19.150
Next time, "The Witching Hour."

00:22:17.650 --> 00:22:20.550
<b>Next Episode</b>

00:22:17.650 --> 00:22:20.550
<b>The Witching Hour</b>
